course: dictionary
lesson: 1423
title: Slang and special words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one place, or to a casual, everyday way of talking. «Every country also has its own jokey words: an English person "popped his clogs", an Australian "carked it", an American "cashed in his chips".» Each of these means someone has died, but each sounds different and says it in a playful way.
explain: Use the clues around cark. In «the old dog finally carked it», the word finally and the idea of an old dog tell you something sad has happened: it died. Cark can also mean stopped working, as in «my phone carked it». Test your meaning by swapping it in: "my phone died" still makes sense for a phone.
explain: Cark it is slang from Australia and New Zealand, so it is casual and a little rough. Do not use it about a person who has just died; say "passed away" instead. Cark also has an old, rare meaning of worry or care, as in "carking cares", but you will hardly ever meet it.
fact: Cark is a verb: cark, carks, carked, carking.
fact: «Cark it» is Australian and New Zealand slang for «died» or «stopped working».
fact: «Carked it» means died; it is Australian slang.
fact: «Chur» is New Zealand slang for «thanks».
fact: Slang is informal language, so check the place and the people before you use it.
exercise: «The old dog finally carked it.» What does carked it mean here? | Ate its dinner | *Died | Ran away | Went to sleep for hours
exercise: «My phone carked it.» What does carked it mean here? | Got a new case | Was sent to the shop for a check | Rang very loudly | *Stopped working
exercise: Which is the polite way to say that a grandparent has just died? | Carked it | Cashed in his chips | Popped his clogs | *Passed away
test: Which form of cark is the past tense? | cark | carking | carks | *carked
test: In the old phrase «carking cares», what does carking mean? | A type of boat | A happy party | *Worry or care | A loud shout
exam: Which statement about cark is correct? | Cark it is polite to use about a person who has just died | Cark is a noun for a type of dog | *Cark it is slang from Australia and New Zealand, so it is casual and a little rough | Cark means happy in every country
